{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And to rule over the day and over the night</b> — The concept of dominion is a different matter from the light-giving that he mentioned, for it includes what he said at the beginning, \"and they shall be for signs and for seasons,\" since they have dominion over the earth through the changes they produce in it, and dominion over generation and loss in all the lower beings: the sun, in its dominion by day, causes growth and generation and increase in all hot and dry things, and the moon, in its dominion, brings forth in springs and seas and all moist and cold things. Therefore he said simply, \"and to rule over the day and over the night\" — for the dominion over the lower beings belongs to them. And it is possible that the dominion is also a power of nobility, that they are the guides of the lower beings and by their power every ruler rules — and the constellation that grows by day rules over it, and the constellation that grows by night rules over it — as in the matter written (Deuteronomy 4:19), \"which YHWH your Elohim has allotted to all the peoples,\" and this is what Scripture says (Psalms 147:4), \"He counts the number of the stars, to all them He calls names,\" and likewise, \"to all them by name He calls\" (Isaiah 40:26), for the calling of names is the distinction of their powers: to this one the power of justice and righteousness, and to that one the power of blood and the sword, and so with all the powers as is known in astrology — and all is by the power of the Most High and according to His will. Therefore he said, \"Great is our Master and abundant in power\" (Psalms 147:5), for He is great over all of them and abundant in power over them, and likewise he said, \"From abundance of might and strong in power\" (Isaiah 40:26). And by way of the sod that I hinted to you, it shall be complete dominion in truth." "<b>And to distinguish between the light and between the darkness</b> — Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 1:18) said that when the sun goes forth by day and the light of the moon by night, they distinguish between the light and between the darkness. But in my opinion, the \"light\" mentioned here is the day, and the \"darkness\" is the night, for so are they named, as he said (Genesis 1:5), \"and Elohim called the light Day and the darkness He called Night.\" Now, throughout the account of Creation, Scripture mentions the command and then narrates the deed, and here too He commanded, \"and they shall be for lights\" (above, v. 15), and narrated, \"and Elohim set them\" (above, v. 17), and said, \"and to rule over the day and over the night\" — that this one shall rule by day and this one by night. And the dominion is what He commanded: \"and they shall be for signs and for seasons\" (above, v. 14), and He narrated that the dominion of the two is not equal, but rather \"to distinguish between the light and between the darkness\" — for the greater one shall rule by day and it shall be entirely light, even in a place where the sun does not reach, and the smaller one shall rule by night and there shall be darkness save for the moon, which illuminates its darkness. And this is the command that he stated: \"to distinguish between the day and between the night\" (v. 14), just as \"and Elohim distinguished between the light and between the darkness\" (above, v. 4)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The first citation from Psalms 147:4 reads לכולם שמות יקרא in the source text; I have preserved Ramban's Hebrew quotation of the verse directly rather than the standard Masoretic text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-21T22:14:23.799108398Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}